<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-eigen-values-vectors-verify">
<title>Verifying eigenvectors and eigenvalues</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case,
	use matrix multiplication to verify that <m>\uvec{x}</m> is an eigenvector for matrix <m>A</m> corresponding to the eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A = \begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				-8 \amp 14 \\
				-7 \amp 13
			\end{abmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\quad
			\uvec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\quad
			\lambda = 6
		</m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Calculate:
		<md>
			<mrow>
				A \uvec{x} \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					-8 \amp 14 \\
					-7 \amp 13
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
				\amp
				6 \uvec{x} \amp =
				6 \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
			</mrow>
			<mrow>
				\amp = \begin{bmatrix} 12 \\ 12 \end{bmatrix} \text{,}
				\amp
				\amp = \begin{bmatrix} 12 \\ 12 \end{bmatrix} \text{.}
			</mrow>
		</md>
		Since the two results match,
		<m>A \uvec{x} = \lambda \uvec{x}</m> is true in this case for <m>\lambda = 6</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A = \begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				-5 \amp 5 \\
				 0 \amp 0
			\end{abmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\quad
			\uvec{x} = \begin{abmatrix}{r} -3 \\ 0 \end{abmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\quad
			\lambda = -5
		</m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Calculate:
		<md>
			<mrow>
				A \uvec{x} \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					-5 \amp 5 \\
					 0 \amp 0
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{abmatrix}{r} -3 \\ 0 \end{abmatrix}
				\amp
				-5 \uvec{x} \amp =
				-5 \begin{abmatrix}{r} -3 \\ 0 \end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>
			<mrow>
				\amp = \begin{bmatrix} 15 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \text{,}
				\amp
				\amp = \begin{bmatrix} 15 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \text{.}
			</mrow>
		</md>
		Since the two results match,
		<m>A \uvec{x} = \lambda \uvec{x}</m> is true in this case for <m>\lambda = -5</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A = \begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				-4 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\
				 0 \amp 2 \amp  0 \\
				 6 \amp 0 \amp  5
			\end{abmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\quad
			\uvec{x} = \begin{abmatrix}{r} -1 \\ 3 \\ 2 \end{abmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\quad
			\lambda = 2
		</m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Calculate:
		<md>
			<mrow>
				A \uvec{x} \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					-4 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\
					 0 \amp 2 \amp  0 \\
					 6 \amp 0 \amp  5
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{abmatrix}{r} -1 \\ 3 \\ 2 \end{abmatrix}
				\amp
				2 \uvec{x} \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{r} -1 \\ 3 \\ 2 \end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>
			<mrow>
				\amp = \begin{abmatrix}{r} -2 \\ 6 \\ 4 \end{abmatrix} \text{,}
				\amp
				\amp = \begin{abmatrix}{r} -2 \\ 6 \\ 4 \end{abmatrix} \text{.}
			</mrow>
		</md>
		Since the two results match,
		<m>A \uvec{x} = \lambda \uvec{x}</m> is true in this case for <m>\lambda = 2</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A = \begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				 1 \amp  1 \amp -1 \amp -1 \\
				-1 \amp  0 \amp -1 \amp  0 \\
				 0 \amp -1 \amp  2 \amp  1 \\
				-1 \amp  1 \amp -3 \amp -1
			\end{abmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\quad
			\uvec{x} = \begin{abmatrix}{r} -3 \\ 4 \\ 3 \\ -2 \end{abmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\quad
			\lambda = 0
		</m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Calculate:
		<md>
			A \uvec{x} =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				 1 \amp  1 \amp -1 \amp -1 \\
				-1 \amp  0 \amp -1 \amp  0 \\
				 0 \amp -1 \amp  2 \amp  1 \\
				-1 \amp  1 \amp -3 \amp -1
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{r} -3 \\ 4 \\ 3 \\ -2 \end{abmatrix}
			=
			\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
		</md>
		Clearly we also have <m>0 \uvec{x} = \zerovec</m>,
		and so <m>A \uvec{x} = \lambda \uvec{x}</m> is true in this case for <m>\lambda = 0</m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
